  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x+1)+({x}^{2}-2x-1)=2{x}^{2}-x,\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a trinomial (it's a binomial).\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+3x+1)+(3{x}^{2}-x-5)=4{x}^{2}+2x-4,\n$$\n\nwhich is a trinomial.\n\n- Statement III is true. The sum of two trinomials *could be* a trinomial (see, for example, statement II). \n\n However, the sum of two trinomials is *not always* a trinomial (see, for example, statement I). \n\n So, the sum of two trinomials is not necessarily a trinomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
